<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Silent Night</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">words <a href="/years/1816">1816</a>, music <a href="/years/1818">1818</a>; first performed on Christmas Eve <a href="/years/1818">1818</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Stille Nacht, heilige Nacht</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph Mohr, a young Catholic priest, wrote the poem &quot;Stille Nacht&quot; in 1816, in the aftermath of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, at Mariapfarr in the Lungau region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>, his father's home town, where he worked as an assistant priest. In 1817 he came to Oberndorf, a village on the Salzach river, then in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/silent-night.jpg" alt="Silent Night" width="330" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franz Xaver Gruber (1787–1863) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stille_nacht.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The carol was composed in 1818 by Franz Xaver Gruber, schoolmaster and organist in the nearby village of Arnsdorf, to words by Father Joseph Mohr. On Christmas Eve 1818 Mohr brought the poem to Gruber and asked him to write a melody and guitar accompaniment for that night's mass, after river flooding had possibly damaged the church organ. The church was later destroyed by repeated flooding and replaced by the Silent-Night-Chapel. &quot;Stille Nacht&quot; was first performed on Christmas Eve 1818 at the Nikolauskirche, the parish church of Oberndorf. Several films repeat the story of a broken organ, which appeared in a fictional account published in the U.S. in the 1930s.</p><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> in Mohr's handwriting, found in 1995 and dated by researchers to about 1820, shows that he wrote the words in 1816 at the pilgrim church of Mariapfarr and that Gruber composed the music in 1818. It is the earliest manuscript known. Before its discovery the carol was often attributed to better-known composers such as Haydn, Mozart and Schubert.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Mohr's poem had six verses. The melody Gruber wrote was a moderato tune in 6/8 time with a siciliana rhythm. The melody changed slightly in the early decades of its spread, and the slow, meditative lullaby or pastorale sung today differs from Gruber's original, especially in its final strain. In 1859 the Episcopal priest John Freeman Young, then at Trinity Church in New York City, published the English translation sung most often today, made from three of the original six verses. The carol has been translated into about 300 languages. Its words and melody are in the public domain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Karl Mauracher, an organ builder who serviced the Oberndorf organ, took the song home to the Zillertal, where two families of travelling folk singers, the Strassers and the Rainers, added it to their shows. The Rainers were singing it by about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> 1819 and performed it before Franz I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I of Russia</a>. They gave its first performance in the U.S., in New York City in 1839. By the 1840s it was known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a> and was reported to be a favourite of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-iv-of-prussia">Frederick William IV of Prussia</a>. The first edition appeared in 1833 in a collection of Four Genuine Tyrolean Songs.</p><p>The carol was first recorded in 1905, and more than 137,000 recordings are known. Composers who have arranged it include Carl Reinecke, John Rutter and David Willcocks, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> declared it an intangible cultural heritage in 2011.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Silent Night” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
